  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)",
